<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Blood River</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1838">1838</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Ncome</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Blood River was fought on 16 December 1838 on the Ncome River in Natal between about 470 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voortrekkers">Voortrekkers</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andries-pretorius">Andries Pretorius</a> and a much larger army of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a>. Defending a wagon laager with firearms, the Voortrekkers killed some 3,000 Zulu without losing a man. The victory led to the founding of the Natalia Republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-blood-river.jpg" alt="Battle of Blood River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Renier Maritz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bloedrivier_monument.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>On 6 February 1838, two days after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piet-retief">Piet Retief</a> and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a> signed a land settlement at UmGungundlovu, Dingane invited Retief's party to a farewell, accused them of witchcraft and had them killed. His regiments then attacked Trekker encampments by night, killing an estimated 500 men, women, children and servants, most notably at Bloukrans. A mounted commando failed at Italeni in April, while a wagon laager held off General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndlela-kasompisi">Ndlela kaSompisi</a>'s 10,000 men at Veglaer in August. The Trekkers then called on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andries-pretorius">Andries Pretorius</a>, appointed commander on 26 November 1838 with 64 wagons.</p><p>Pretorius backed Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpande">Mpande</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a>'s half-brother, who had fled with 17,000 followers. On 6 December, after meeting friendly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> chiefs at Danskraal, he proposed a vow that the commando and its descendants would celebrate the victory and build a church if they succeeded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Avoiding the narrow defile at Italeni, Pretorius halted on 15 December and formed a laager beside a deep hippo pool in the Ncome River, which guarded two sides, with open ground in front. Movable barriers closed the gaps between the wheels, and two small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, one a 6-pound carronade named Grietjie, fired grapeshot. During the night six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> regiments, an estimated 20,000 or more, crossed the river, while Ndlela and his elite troops stayed on the far bank.</p><p>At dawn on 16 December the regiments under Dambuza stormed the laager repeatedly but could not break through. After two hours and four waves, Pretorius sent out mounted men, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> scattered, and the Trekkers pursued them for three hours. In the Zulu telling, only the left horn of the army made contact, advancing under the mist. The historian S. P. Mackenzie doubts the reported number of Zulu dead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>UmGungundlovu was found deserted and burnt, and the bones of Retief and his men were buried there. The war went on: eleven days after the battle a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> prisoner led a Trekker party into a trap at the White Umfolozi River. Dingane was finally defeated in January 1840 at Maqongqe once <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpande">Mpande</a> joined the Trekkers, and Pretorius attended Mpande's crowning. For <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> nationalism the battle came to signify God's intervention, and 16 December, once the Day of the Vow, is now the Day of Reconciliation. Monuments to both sides stand on either bank of the river.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Blood River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1838 Zulu forces fought the Voortrekkers, and at the <b>Battle of Blood River</b> on 16 December a Boer laager under Andries Pretorius repulsed Dingane’s army with heavy losses.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After Dingane's men killed Piet Retief's party in 1838, Andries Pretorius led the commando that defeated the Zulu at <b>Blood River</b> in December 1838.</blockquote><a href="/state/natalia-republic">Natalia Republic</a></li></ul></section>
